WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:06.200
In 1893, a young Indian man left his home to earn a living in South Africa.

2
00:00:06.201 --> 00:00:14.232
Soon after he arrived, he needed to travel to the capital, so his employer purchased a first-class train ticket for him.

3
00:00:14.233 --> 00:00:25.672
Mohandas Gandhi took his seat in the first-class compartment, but at one of the stations along the way, a white passenger boarded the train and refused to share the compartment with an Indian.

4
00:00:25.673 --> 00:00:30.654
The white man called the conductor, who told Gandhi to go to the luggage car.

5
00:00:30.655 --> 00:00:34.647
In his autobiography, Gandhi describes what happened:

6
00:00:34.648 --> 00:00:38.543
"But, I have a first-class ticket," Gandhi protested.

7
00:00:38.544 --> 00:00:43.601
"That doesn't matter," the conductor replied. "You must go to the luggage compartment."

8
00:00:43.602 --> 00:00:51.530
"I tell you, I was permitted at Durban to travel in this compartment," Gandhi objected, "and I insist on staying in it."

9
00:00:51.531 --> 00:00:59.190
When the conductor realized that Gandhi would not move, he called a police officer, and together, they threw him off the train.

10
00:00:59.191 --> 00:01:03.919
This incident was the spark that began Gandhi's political activism.

11
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:14.588
Humiliated and left by the side of the railroad tracks, Gandhi would one day lead India to independence and inspire movements for civil rights and freedom across the world.

12
00:01:14.589 --> 00:01:18.394
Gandhi was born on October 2, 1869.

13
00:01:18.395 --> 00:01:24.026
His parents were faithful Hindus and part of a well-respected caste of traders. The

14
00:01:24.027 --> 00:01:29.073
caste system divided India's population into different social classes. Some

15
00:01:29.074 --> 00:01:36.000
castes were held in high esteem; some were considered so low that their members were called "untouchables." As

16
00:01:36.001 --> 00:01:39.727
a young man, Gandhi went to study law in London. Members

17
00:01:39.728 --> 00:01:47.870
of his caste, however, believed that by going to London, Gandhi was abandoning his faith, and they expelled him from the caste. While

18
00:01:47.871 --> 00:01:55.099
studying, Gandhi held true to his Hindu beliefs, but when he returned home, people avoided him anyway. He

19
00:01:55.100 --> 00:02:01.550
found he could not earn a living as a lawyer in India, so he decided to try his luck in South Africa. South

20
00:02:01.551 --> 00:02:09.311
Africa had a large Indian community, and Gandhi was able to find work in the city of Durban in Natal province. Unfortunately,

21
00:02:09.312 --> 00:02:14.523
discrimination against Indians was common in South Africa. Indians

22
00:02:14.524 --> 00:02:21.006
could not leave their homes after 9:00 p.m., and businesses commonly refused to serve Indians. During

23
00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:27.284
Gandhi's time there, the government prepared legislation that would deny Indians the right to vote. Later,

24
00:02:27.285 --> 00:02:32.783
the government even decided to declare all Hindu marriages invalid. In

25
00:02:32.784 --> 00:02:39.781
1894, Gandhi founded the Natal Indian Congress in an effort to combat discrimination. For

26
00:02:39.782 --> 00:02:49.729
the next 12 years, Gandhi was politically active in South Africa, but he found that his attempts to protect Indians from prejudice largely failed. Letters,

27
00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:52.829
petitions, and protests did not help. Gandhi

28
00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:57.539
decided that the strategy behind the Indians' protests must change. He

29
00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:04.371
asked his protesters to embrace the idea of ahimsa ─a Hindu word meaning "nonviolence."

30
00:03:04.372 --> 00:03:11.982
Gandhi realized that if Indians engaged in violent protest, the protest itself would be a reason for more oppression.

31
00:03:11.983 --> 00:03:17.965
Gandhi called his new method of protest satyagraha , or "devotion to

32
00:03:17.966 --> 00:03:25.019
truth." When faced with an unjust law, Gandhi and his followers would openly break the law, but not resist

33
00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:34.283
arrest. In 1906, when the government required all Indians to carry identity cards, Gandhi led his people in a satyagraha

34
00:03:34.284 --> 00:03:41.831
protest. South Africa's Indian citizens peacefully went on strike, refused to register, and burned their identity

35
00:03:41.832 --> 00:03:44.835
cards. Thousands were imprisoned, including

36
00:03:44.836 --> 00:03:47.510
Gandhi. Others were beaten, and some were

37
00:03:47.511 --> 00:03:57.062
shot. After seven years of brutally mistreating a population determined to be peaceful, the government faced a public outcry and was forced to

38
00:03:57.063 --> 00:04:01.662
compromise. The South African government had been shamed into doing the right

39
00:04:01.663 --> 00:04:09.161
thing. When Gandhi returned to India in 1914, he arrived with the reputation of an influential political

40
00:04:09.162 --> 00:04:15.088
figure. He had also built a reputation as a spiritual leader who was deeply committed to

41
00:04:15.089 --> 00:04:26.530
peace. During his time in South Africa, whenever protesters fought back or resisted violently, Gandhi went on a hunger strike until his followers returned to the values of ahimsa

42
00:04:26.531 --> 00:04:31.360
. In India, he continued his fight against oppression and unjust

43
00:04:31.361 --> 00:04:39.471
rule. At the time, India was ruled by the British. Many landless farmers worked for British landlords who paid them very

44
00:04:39.472 --> 00:04:46.018
little. They forced farmers to grow cash crops for export when the farmers themselves were desperately

45
00:04:46.019 --> 00:04:51.398
hungry. When Gandhi organized strikes against foreign landowners, he was quickly

46
00:04:51.399 --> 00:04:55.560
arrested. Thousands of his supporters protested, and the government

47
00:04:55.561 --> 00:05:02.599
yielded. They released Gandhi, and farmers were given more control over the land they farmed so that they could feed their

48
00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:09.432
families. After this, many Indians began to refer to Gandhi as Mahatma, which means "great

49
00:05:09.433 --> 00:05:16.105
soul." Gandhi believed that the lives of Indians would not truly improve unless India gained

50
00:05:16.106 --> 00:05:27.141
independence. The political party most active in India's independence movement was the Congress Party, and although Gandhi never officially joined it, he became its unofficial spiritual

51
00:05:27.142 --> 00:05:33.910
leader. Over the next years, Gandhi led nonviolent protests designed to make British rule over India

52
00:05:33.911 --> 00:05:39.312
difficult. One of Gandhi's most famous protests was the Salt March of

53
00:05:39.313 --> 00:05:48.028
1930. Anyone in India who made salt was required to pay a tax, and Gandhi considered these "salt laws" to be a symbol of the injustice of British

54
00:05:48.029 --> 00:05:56.847
rule. Salt is a necessity of life and something that Indians could easily make or collect for free, but the salt laws forced them to pay for

55
00:05:56.848 --> 00:06:01.891
it. To protest this law, Gandhi began a march to the coastal town of

56
00:06:01.892 --> 00:06:08.335
Dandi. The 400─kilometer walk took him 28 days, and thousands joined him on his way to the

57
00:06:08.336 --> 00:06:14.104
sea. When they arrived, Gandhi walked to the edge of the ocean and made salt─an illegal

58
00:06:14.105 --> 00:06:22.318
act. The crowd followed his example of peaceful lawbreaking, and as the salt protest spread, over 60,000 were

59
00:06:22.319 --> 00:06:27.935
arrested. If the British wanted to rule India, they would have to imprison tens of thousands of

60
00:06:27.936 --> 00:06:36.863
people. When Britain entered World War II in 1939, it unilaterally included India in the war without consulting India's

61
00:06:36.864 --> 00:06:44.972
leaders. Gandhi, however, believed his country could not help the British fight for their freedom when Britain was denying freedom to the Indian

62
00:06:44.973 --> 00:06:51.970
people. Unless India was granted immediate independence, the most Indians could offer was nonviolent moral

63
00:06:51.971 --> 00:07:00.129
support. As the war progressed, Gandhi increased his demands for independence, drafting a resolution calling for the British to "quit

64
00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:02.955
India." The Quit India movement became

65
00:07:02.956 --> 00:07:12.998
massive. Unfortunately, sometimes ahimsa was forgotten in the excitement; both sides committed violent acts during the years of this struggle, and these troubled Gandhi

66
00:07:12.999 --> 00:07:19.602
deeply. Thousands died and hundreds of thousands were arrested, including Gandhi and the leaders of the Congress Party.

67
00:07:19.603 --> 00:07:26.775
The British held Gandhi for two years to separate him from his followers, but this strategy was not effective.

68
00:07:26.776 --> 00:07:32.775
Gandhi had become a symbol of Indian independence, and the protests only continued to grow.

69
00:07:32.776 --> 00:07:38.320
At the end of the war, the British announced that India would become an independent nation.

70
00:07:38.321 --> 00:07:45.358
Gandhi called off the struggle, and the Congress leadership and over 100,000 political prisoners were released.

71
00:07:45.359 --> 00:07:51.357
India became independent from British rule on August 15, 1947.

72
00:07:51.358 --> 00:08:00.144
India was now free, but religious and ethnic differences led to the partition of the country, creating the state of Pakistan.

73
00:08:00.145 --> 00:08:05.023
Despite the partition, Gandhi worked tirelessly to promote peace.

74
00:08:05.024 --> 00:08:11.743
Sadly, on January 30, 1948, Gandhi was murdered by a religious extremist.

75
00:08:11.744 --> 00:08:21.555
Gandhi's life and teachings have inspired leaders of freedom struggles around the world, including the civil rights movements in the United States and South Africa.

76
00:08:21.556 --> 00:08:33.003
Albert Einstein also believed that we should strive to follow in his footsteps: we should not use violence in fighting for our cause, and refuse to participate in anything we believe is evil.

77
00:08:33.004 --> 00:08:40.259
After Gandhi's death he said, "Generations to come will scarcely believe that such a one as this walked the earth."

78
00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:43.483
Mahatma Gandhi truly was a great soul.

